# Files the Cursor agent shouldn't see.
#
# What this actually does: anything matched below stays out of Agent, Tab,
# Inline Edit, and @-mention context. It's path matching, gitignore syntax. It
# has no idea what's inside a file — only where the file sits.
#
# Don't confuse this with .cursorindexingignore sitting next to it. That one is
# noise reduction: files stay readable, they just stop cluttering search. This
# one is access.
#
# On the data — these are synthetic HL7 spec examples, not real PHI. They live
# where real patient extracts live in the production repo, so the patterns carry
# over even though the data doesn't.
#
# If you edit this: a pattern matching nothing blocks nothing, and nothing will
# warn you. git check-ignore is no help, git has no idea this file exists and
# will cheerfully report "not ignored" for something Cursor is blocking. The
# only honest test is to @-mention a file and watch it get refused. Widen
# freely. Don't narrow it without a second pair of eyes.
# Bulk patient records. Flat, many-records-per-file — the shape a real clinical
# export actually arrives in, which is why these are first on the list.
src/Microsoft.Health.Fhir.Tests.Common/TestFiles/**/*.ndjson

# Patient instances. Deliberately not Patient profiles or StructureDefinitions —
# the agent needs those to write conformant code, and they describe shape rather
# than carrying data. Three directories hold instances today; if a fourth turns
# up, add it, because the rule is about the kind of data and not the location.
# [Pp] is there because casing is inconsistent across the R4/Stu3/Normative sets.
src/Microsoft.Health.Fhir.Tests.Common/TestFiles/**/[Pp]atient*.json
src/Microsoft.Health.Fhir.Tests.Common/TestFiles/**/[Pp]atient*.xml
src/Microsoft.Health.Fhir.Tests.Common/TestFiles/**/Smart[Pp]atient*.json
src/Microsoft.Health.Fhir.Tests.Common/TestFiles/**/TokenOverflow[Pp]atient*.json
docs/rest/Data/*[Pp]atient*.json

# Local dev credentials. launchSettings.json carries local SQL connection
# strings, and the appsettings overlays carry per-environment config. Base
# appsettings.json is deliberately left alone — see below.
testauthenvironment.json
**/launchSettings.json
**/appsettings.*.json

# Helm secret templates.
samples/kubernetes/helm/**/*secret*.yaml

# Left readable on purpose — these are calls, not gaps:
#
#   imagingstudy-example.json      Not patient data, and VERA-1042 needs it. A
#                                  boundary that blocks the work it exists to
#                                  protect is a boundary people delete.
#   appsettings.json (base)        Connection string is empty. Genuine config
#                                  context the agent needs to reason about setup.
#   docs/rest/**/Profiles/*.json   StructureDefinitions, not instances. Describe
#                                  shape, contain no data. Note that
#                                  US_Core_Profiles/Patient.json is a profile
#                                  despite the filename.
#   CredScanSuppressions.json      The "mySecretPassword123" in there is a
#                                  documentation placeholder in a suppression
#                                  list, not a credential.
#
# One thing this can't fix: roughly 50 Bundle fixtures under TestFiles/ may
# contain Patient entries, and path matching can't see inside a file. That's a
# limit of the mechanism rather than something to pattern around — and it's a
# large part of why the CI check exists.